  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Windy start to the week with light rain in the\nnorthern tier\n\nAnother upper level trough and weak sfc cold front will bring\nlight rain mainly to the northern tier of CPA later tonight \nthrough Monday morning. A steady breeze from the west/southwest \nwill shift to the NW and ramp higher into Monday afternoon with\npeaks gusts up to 40 mph. Breezy conditions will continue\nthrough the first part of the week with temperatures rebounding\nmodestly higher by Tuesday. No additional precip is forecast\nthrough midweek. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: First accumulating snow of the season possible \nfor late week\n\nThere is a growing model signal for a substantial long wave \ntrough to carve out over the eastern U.S. by the end of the \nweek. An amplifying shortwave embedded within the deep upper\ntrough trough should drive a sfc low across the region Thursday\nand could develop into a potential nor'easter as it tracks \noffshore. While the the low track and expected impacts are \nstill not clear, precip prospects continue to increase for late \nin the week. The most likely scenario at this point would lean \ntoward a widespread area of rain Thursday mixing/changing to \nsnow across the higher elevations Thursday night into Friday. \nCold air expanding southward behind an increasing NW wind could\ndrive lake effect and upslope precip Friday night into the \nweekend. Friday and Saturday are fcst to be the coldest days \nwith temperatures 5-10 degrees below normal.",
